{"hash":"3ee312e04900270d","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":88.773,"said":"Here sits Nandi, Shiva’s bull, gazing at the door. Now for the five brothers. They were the Pandavas, heroes of the Mahabharata. They had won the great war at Kurukshetra, but to win it they had killed their own kin, and the weight of it would not leave them. So they came looking for Shiva, to ask his forgiveness. Shiva did not wish to be found so easily. Tradition says he slipped away from them, hiding first at Guptakashi, the hidden Kashi, down the valley, and then here, among the herds on these slopes, in the form of a buffalo; many in these hills tell it as a bull. Bhima, the strongest brother, saw through it. He stood astride two hills, and the animals passed beneath him, all but one. Bhima sprang at it, and as it sank into the earth, he caught hold of its back. The Shiva Purana says the head came to rest far away in Nepal, while the body stayed here. Garhwal tradition adds four more places, together the Panch Kedar, where the arms, the face, the navel and the hair appeared. But the hump stayed here, and Shiva, moved by their devotion, forgave them. Now climb the steps into the hall.\n\nDid you know? The Panch Kedar are Kedarnath for the hump, Tungnath for the arms, Rudranath for the face, Madhyamaheshwar for the navel and Kalpeshwar for the hair.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1105},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1121,"end":1270}],"words":[[0.098,0.3,0,4],[0.411,0.325,5,9],[0.747,0.493,10,15],[1.456,0.481,17,24],[1.949,0.361,25,29],[2.55,0.481,31,37],[3.043,0.144,38,40],[3.199,0.096,41,44],[3.307,0.409,45,49],[4.663,0.361,51,54],[5.036,0.144,55,58],[5.192,0.072,59,62],[5.276,0.337,63,67],[5.625,0.565,68,76],[7.079,0.18,78,82],[7.272,0.12,83,87],[7.404,0.084,88,91],[7.5,0.637,92,100],[8.317,0.457,102,108],[8.786,0.096,109,111],[8.894,0.096,112,115],[9.002,0.685,116,127],[10.589,0.144,129,133],[10.745,0.18,134,137],[10.938,0.264,138,141],[11.214,0.084,142